Why does an int variable that multiplies by a double still type as an int?

I was exploring types of variables and cannot understand why, when getting to the last two lines of the following code, the variable i types as an int. I think I understand type coercion but I thought that the type that takes precedence is the larger type. Have I understood this wrong? And why does i show up as an int if it is multiplied by a double?

Code:

void some_function() 
{
    double d = 2.2;
    cout << "1: double d: " << d << " type is: " << typeid(d).name() << "\n";
    int i = 7;
    cout << "2: int i: " << i << " type is: " << typeid(i).name() << "\n";
    d = d + i;
    cout << "3: double d plus int i: " << d << " type is: " << typeid(d).name() << "\n";
    i = d * i;
    cout << "4: d * i: " << i << " type is: " << typeid(i).name() << "\n";
}

Output:

1: double d: 2.2 type is: double  
2: int i: 7 type is: int  
3: double d plus int i: 9.2 type is: double  
4: d * i: 64 type is: int

