iPhone Duo: tab bar briefly moves right after dismissing a fullScreen modal

Xcode 27.1 (27A9275), iPhone Duo simulator, iOS 27.1, folded portrait.

Tap Present, then Close. Expected: horizontal bars throughout. Actual: the tab bar briefly moves from the bottom to the right, shrinking Home’s safe area, then returns. Both navigation controllers use .disabled and return nil for child forwarding.

How can I prevent this while keeping .fullScreen and UIKit’s automatic appearance callbacks? .overFullScreen changes those callbacks; forcing bar updates/layout did not help.

Single-file UIKit app (deployment target iOS 27.1, scene lifecycle enabled, no storyboards):

import UIKit

@main
final class AppDelegate: UIResponder, UIApplicationDelegate {
    func application(_ application: UIApplication,
                     configurationForConnecting session: UISceneSession,
                     options: UIScene.ConnectionOptions) -> UISceneConfiguration {
        let configuration = UISceneConfiguration(name: nil, sessionRole: session.role)
        configuration.delegateClass = SceneDelegate.self
        return configuration
    }
}

final class SceneDelegate: UIResponder, UIWindowSceneDelegate {
    var window: UIWindow?

    func scene(_ scene: UIScene, willConnectTo session: UISceneSession,
               options: UIScene.ConnectionOptions) {
        guard let scene = scene as? UIWindowScene else { return }
        let home = HorizontalNavigationController(rootViewController: Page())
        home.tabBarItem = UITabBarItem(title: "Home", image: UIImage(systemName: "house"), tag: 0)
        let other = HorizontalNavigationController(rootViewController: UIViewController())
        other.tabBarItem = UITabBarItem(title: "Other", image: UIImage(systemName: "star"), tag: 1)
        let tabs = UITabBarController()
        tabs.viewControllers = [home, other]
        let window = UIWindow(windowScene: scene)
        window.rootViewController = tabs
        self.window = window
        window.makeKeyAndVisible()
    }
}

final class HorizontalNavigationController: UINavigationController {
    override var preferredVerticalBarBehavior: UIVerticalBarBehavior { .disabled }
    override var childForPreferredVerticalBarBehavior: UIViewController? { nil }
}

final class Page: UIViewController {
    var isModal = false

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        title = isModal ? "Full screen" : "Home"
        view.backgroundColor = .systemBackground
        navigationItem.rightBarButtonItem = UIBarButtonItem(
            title: isModal ? "Close" : "Present", style: .plain,
            target: self, action: #selector(Page.performAction))

        let box = UIView()
        box.backgroundColor = isModal ? .systemIndigo : .systemTeal
        box.layer.borderColor = UIColor.systemYellow.cgColor
        box.layer.borderWidth = 4
        box.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        view.addSubview(box)
        let safe = view.safeAreaLayoutGuide
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            box.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: safe.topAnchor),
            box.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: safe.bottomAnchor),
            box.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: safe.leadingAnchor),
            box.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: safe.trailingAnchor)
        ])
        let label = UILabel()
        label.text = isModal ? "Tap Close" : "Safe area"
        label.textColor = .white
        label.font = .systemFont(ofSize: 28)
        label.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        box.addSubview(label)
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            label.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: box.centerXAnchor),
            label.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: box.centerYAnchor)
        ])
    }

    @objc private func performAction() {
        if isModal {
            dismiss(animated: true)
        } else {
            let page = Page()
            page.isModal = true
            let modal = HorizontalNavigationController(rootViewController: page)
            modal.modalPresentationStyle = .fullScreen
            present(modal, animated: true)
        }
    }
}

How to safely manipulate or inspect stack frames in C++ using inline assembly without triggering Undefined Behavior?

I am writing a low-level routine in C++ that mixes inline assembly (or an external .s file) to inspect or modify the current stack frame.

Here is an example:

#include <iostream>

extern "C" void custom_stack_inspect();

void target_function() {
    int x = 42;
    custom_stack_inspect();
}

int main() {
    target_function();
    return 0;
}

And the assembly:

.global custom_stack_inspect
custom_stack_inspect:
    # What is the portable or ABI-compliant way to peek at caller's local 'x' here?
    ret

In x86_64 (System V vs. MSVC ABI), how does the compiler guarantee where local variables like x live relative to rbp or rsp, especially when optimizations (-O2 / -O3) eliminate the base pointer?

What exact C++ rules or Undefined Behavior clauses are violated if an assembly routine manually walks up the stack frame chain?

