Fillable PDF unfillable in Acrobat after being modified by PdfSharp library, but remains fillable in web browsers

I'm having trouble with the outputs of the PdfSharp library.

A new feature in my application downloads a fillable PDF from our server, fills it with information based on user input, and saves it to the local disk. I was able to wrangle PdfSharp into accepting info and saving the PDF in a still-fillable form.

However, one issue persists. When the PDF is opened in a browser (I tried Firefox and Chromium-based), the fields are still editable. However, when the file is opened in Adobe Acrobat, the fields are non-editable.

I've uploaded an example of my test case in which the user information has been replaced with a simple incrementing number: Example.pdf

In my code, I am explicitly allowing Modifying and FormsFill:

PdfSharp.Pdf.PdfDocument pdfDocument = PdfReader.Open(myStream, PdfDocumentOpenMode.Modify);
pdfDocument.SecuritySettings.PermitModifyDocument = true;
pdfDocument.SecuritySettings.PermitFormsFill = true;
pdfDocument.Options.CompressContentStreams = false;

I have also explicitly set all AcroForm fields to ReadOnly = false:

foreach (string iter in acroFormKeys)
{
    if (acroFormFieldCollection[iter] != null)
    {
        PdfAcroField genericField = acroFormFieldCollection[iter];
        genericField.ReadOnly = false;
    }
}

My application uses PdfSharp 6.2.4, programmed in C# in a .NET Framework 4.8 environment. I have not tried this with PdfSharp 6.5.0 for the simple reason that I got everything else working in 6.2.4 and it might break if I update.

Since the resulting PDF is editable in the browsers, I believe the settings are being respected. I suppose the root question is, is there some other setting or option that will allow the resulting PDF to be editable in Adobe Acrobat, or is that simply Adobe recognizing that I'm using a free library and shutting me down?

I have unsuccessfully searched for this specific question and asked on the PdfSharp forum, but no pay dirt as of yet.

Can setting/retrieving properties on any HTML element be a security risk?

When assigning properties to a HTMLElement directly (so [Element].someProperty), it looks like anything goes.

You would expect that invalid properties either throw some error or are weeded out silently on assigning them.

This seems to be not the case. In the snippet is a minimal example, where after appending an element with a property that is function, that property can be referenced (and even invoked).

Retrieving the value of it using getAttribute null is returned. Retrieving it directly however returns the property value (a function).

I can't get my head around that. Will this constitute a security problem? And if so, how to circumvent it?

