Bug with sockets after timeout : ntp client as example

I wrote a nice NTP client in Perl, getting time info from a pool of servers.

It works perfectly when all servers are available. But if any fail to response, select times out for all subsequent servers too, even though I see the responses coming in using tcpdump.

Is there a bug or wrong manipulation with the select/recv functions when they get called again, after a timeout or bad reply (closed NTP port) ? Should i consume more than the expected 48 bytes and/or reset something ? I'd like to add minimal correction and stay with such clean, minimal, independent code. The original program has an external loop, and works in round-robin fashion (pausing).

Code:

#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;

sub makesa(@) { my $ipz; my ($remhost,$proto,$dport)=@_;
    $dport= (getservbyname($dport,$proto))[2];
    $ipz= ((gethostbyname $remhost)[4])[0];
    if ($ipz) { return pack('Sna4x8',2,$dport,$ipz) } else {return}
}

my @NTP0= qw(pool.ntp.org. ntp.obspm.fr. ntp3.fau.de.);

my $TIMEOUT= 5;
my($k,$ntpr,$ntpserv,$rin,$sa,$sock,$tntp,$tsys, @data,@ntps,%sa2name);
my $NTPQUERY= pack 'B8C3N11','00001011',(0)x14; # NTP request v3

# prep
@ARGV=@NTP0 if $#ARGV<0;
for $_ (@ARGV) {
    if ($sa=makesa($_,'udp','ntp')) { $sa2name{$sa}=$_; push @ntps,$sa }
}
die $! unless socket($sock,2,2,17);
$rin=''; vec($rin,fileno($sock),1)=1;
print "#STRAT\tNTP_SERVER\tDELAY\tNTP_DATE\n";

for ($k=0;$k<=$#ntps;$k++) {
$sa=$ntps[$k]; $ntpserv=$sa2name{$sa};

# query
unless (send($sock,$NTPQUERY,undef,$sa)) { print STDERR "[ERR] $ntpserv: $!\n"; next }
undef $ntpr; # yy required?
if (select($rin,undef,undef,$TIMEOUT)) { recv $sock,$ntpr,48,undef }
$tsys= time(); #yy higher accuracy/Time::HiRes

#yyy!BUG: if a server is down, all next fail ?reason ? reusing socket ? bytes after 48 hanging@select
#$_=unpack('H*',$ntpr); s/../$& /g; print STDERR "[DEBUG] $ntpserv REPONSE:$_\n";

# decode
@data= unpack 'N12',$ntpr; # @data items: 4 bytes, little-endian
next if length($ntpr)!=48 || $data[0]>>24!=0x0c; # response length and status expected
$tntp=$data[10]-2208988800; $tntp+=$data[11]/(1<<32) if $data[11]>0;

print join("\t",0xff&($data[0]>>16),$ntpserv,$tsys-$tntp,scalar(localtime $tntp)),"\n";
}
exit 0;

